{"timestamp": "2026-08-20T08:36:37.970984+00:00", "type": "answer_done", "session_id": "default", "turn_id": 2, "answer": "- I break the problem into the core hypothesis, run a quick A/B test on a minimal viable change, because data‑backed validation keeps the team focused and prevents scope creep.  \n- When stakeholders disagree, I map each view to a measurable KPI—like conversion lift or churn reduction—and run a short experiment, since aligning on outcomes resolves tension faster than endless debate.  \n- If timelines slip, I re‑prioritize the backlog using the RICE scoring model, because quantifying reach, impact, confidence and effort lets us cut low‑value work and meet the critical launch date.  \n- I keep transparent status updates in our sprint board and a daily 5‑minute stand‑up, since visible progress and early flagging of blockers let the team adapt before issues become crises.  \n- After the issue resolves, I conduct a post‑mortem with a fishbone diagram to capture root causes and action items, because turning a challenge into a documented learning prevents repeat failures.", "answer_ms": 1163}
